// Runtime/RuntimeAbort.maxon — ⭐ **THE ONE HOME OF THE PROCESS EXIT CODES THE EMITTED RUNTIME ABORTS
// WITH**, and the one shape those aborts are emitted in.
//
// ⚠ **IT EXISTS BECAUSE THE SPACE HAD ALREADY COLLIDED.** `GtRuntime.ProcStoreOverflowExitCode` and
// `ManagedMemoryRuntime.ElementSizeZeroExitCode` were BOTH 70 — two unrelated aborts wearing one number, each
// picked against a different file-local list of "codes I can see from here". Nothing was broken by it
// (a program cannot reach both), but the next number added was a coin flip, and a runtime abort that
// reports another abort's code is a debugging session that starts by reading the wrong function.
//
// ⭐ **THE FIX IS THE SHAPE THE 4-DIGIT ERROR CODES ALREADY PROVE: ONE DECLARATION SITE PLUS A CHECK
// THAT FAILS THE BUILD ON A DUPLICATE** — except that here the check needs no tooling, because the
// LANGUAGE already is one. An enum's raw values must be distinct, so a second claimant on a number is
// **E3031 `duplicate raw value`** from the compiler that compiles this file: the collision cannot be
// committed, let alone shipped. That is why these are enum CASES and not seven `let`s.
//
// ⚠ **THIS IS NOT THE 4-DIGIT ERROR REGISTRY AND MUST NOT BE FOLDED INTO IT.** `docs/error-codes.txt`
// numbers DIAGNOSTICS the compiler prints at compile time; these are PROCESS EXIT CODES a compiled
// program dies with at run time. Different space, different consumers (a shell / a spec's `exitcode`
// block), different lifetimes.
//
// ⭐ **HOW TO PICK THE NEXT ONE: take the next free number ABOVE 70.** The band below is not ours —
// 0-69 are ordinary program exit codes (a Maxon `main` returns one), and the subprocess specs' children
// already use 1/2/3/4/5/25/45/123 — so an abort down there is indistinguishable from a program that
// simply returned. (`runProcess`'s two failures THROW `RunProcessError` for that reason, and no abort sits
// below 71.)
// 101 is the one code the compiler already spends outside this band and it is declared here so the
// enum's own duplicate check covers it.
//
// ⚠ **134 IS NOT HERE, AND ITS ABSENCE IS DELIBERATE.** A user `panic("…")` routes to the SAME `mrt_panic`
// runtime the range-check guard uses (`StdOp.osPanic`), which exits `PanicExitCode`, so nothing emits 134.
// A case nothing emits is a DEAD CLAIM, the same rot as a code that reports another abort's number — hence
// no entry rather than a number this space defends against a claimant that does not exist.
//
// ⚠⚠ **80 IS NOT HERE EITHER, FOR THAT SAME RULE.** The code it would name — an out-of-range byte read —
// is answered by `stdlib/String.maxon`'s `byteAtOrPanic`, whose body IS
// `try byteAt(index) otherwise panic("String.byteAtOrPanic: index out of bounds — …")`: ordinary Maxon
// reaching `osPanic` with a real line and a real message. Only a SYNTHESIZED runtime function, which has no
// source line and no message to bake, needs a number instead.
// ⇒ **THE NEXT ENTRY TAKES THE NEXT FREE NUMBER ABOVE 70, WHICH IS 120.** 76, 80 and 108 are vacant and stay so:
// reserving a vacated number would be this space defending against a claimant that no longer exists — the
// very thing the paragraph above rejects.
export enum RuntimeAbort
	// `__managed_grow`: `capacity·element_size` overflowed i64. Without the guard the wrapped byte count
	// under-allocates and the next element write runs off the buffer — a silent heap overflow.
	arrayCapacityOverflow = 71

	// `__managed_append`: `dest` and `src` disagree on `element_size@24`, so the single-stride blit would
	// over-read the packed source and splice garbage in.
	arrayAppendElementSizeMismatch = 72

	// `__managed_resize`: a NEGATIVE length. The shrink's zero range starts at `buffer + n·es`, which for a
	// negative `n` is an address BELOW the buffer — the one out-of-buffer write a detach cannot fix.
	// Pinned by `byte-string-literal.negative-resize-aborts`.
	arrayNegativeResize = 73

	// `__managed_create`: a 0 element size, which is a COMPILER bug rather than a program's (a concrete
	// literal is never 0 and a descriptor read never yields 0) — it would make `__managed_grow` allocate a
	// zero-byte buffer and corrupt.
	arrayElementSizeZero = 74

	// `__mm_leak_check` (the ONE OS-exit gate): a green thread was spawned but neither AWAITED nor DROPPED —
	// `__gt_live_count` was non-zero at exit (or, negative, an over-reclaim). A GT record and its seed stack come
	// from the record arena and `osAllocPages`, neither of them a box, so this leak is its own class
	// and its own code — DISTINCT from `leakDetected` (101, the heap gate) so a debugger reading the exit code
	// goes to the green-thread counter, not the heap one (this file's founding rule: an abort must not report
	// another abort's number). Pinned by `async-promise-drop`'s never-ran/completed/parked cases.
	greenThreadLeak = 75

	// ⚠ **76 IS RETIRED, NOT REUSABLE (W105).** It was `hashTableInsertNoFreeSlot` — the shared
	// `__set_insert`/`__map_upsert` probe walking every slot without meeting an Empty one or a reusable
	// tombstone. `HashTableRuntime` is deleted, so nothing emits that `osExit` and a live registry may not go
	// on claiming the number. It is left VACANT rather than handed to the next abort, for this registry's
	// founding reason: an exit code is read by a debugger against whatever the tree said at the time, and a
	// recycled number makes two different faults indistinguishable in an old log. The corpus `Set.insert` /
	// `Map.upsert` reach the same invariant through an ordinary `panic`, with a message and a source line.
	// `__mf_stat_field` / `__mf_stat_free`: a NULL stat buffer. It is a stdlib-only INVARIANT violation
	// rather than a catchable error (`__ManagedFile.statField`/`statFree` are registered non-throwing),
	// and `__mm_free` is not null-safe — without the guard the free writes to the
	// header eight bytes below address 0.
	managedFileStatNullBuffer = 78

	// `__mf_stat_field`: an index outside the six fields the 48-byte stat buffer holds — including a
	// NEGATIVE one, which the signed `StdCmpPred` makes a separate compare. Without the guard the read
	// returns whatever sits past a 48-byte allocation: a silent wrong answer, not a crash.
	managedFileStatIndexOutOfRange = 79

	// `__list_remove_node` / `__list_detach_node`: the node handed in is linked into a DIFFERENT chain than
	// the receiver. `owner@32` names the chain a node belongs to, and an unlink gating on it merely being
	// non-zero lets `b.remove(nodeOfA)` repair `b`'s `head`/`tail`/`count` from `a`'s links — MEASURED as a
	// live chain reading `count = -1` and then **exit 139** on a `String` element (and a silent wrong answer
	// on an `int` one). Nothing in the type system can catch it: two chains of one element type share
	// a `__ManagedListNode` type, so the handle checks out at the call.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT ABORTS BY NUMBER RATHER THAN THROUGH `osPanic`, FOR THIS FILE'S OWN RECORDED REASON** — a
	// synthesized runtime function has no source line and no message to bake (see the `stringByteAtOutOfRange`
	// note above). ⚖ WHAT `list.remove(nodeOfAnother)` SHOULD MEAN — refuse earlier, or deliberately retarget
	// — is a semantic question `/specs/managed-list.md` does not answer and this abort does not decide; it
	// only stops the corruption. The front end refuses every spelling it can see (`dispatchManagedListMethod`'s
	// markability guard catches the inline `a.remove(b.insertLast(x))`), so reaching here needs two named
	// chains and a handle carried between them.
	managedListNodeNotInThisChain = 77

	// `__list_node_value`: the node's element slot is EMPTY on a chain whose element is MANAGED — the state
	// `__list_remove_node`/`__list_remove_first` leave behind when they hand the element to the caller and
	// NULL `value@16` so nobody drops it twice. `__list_node_decref` is built for that state and skips its
	// drop; every OTHER reader of `value@16` is not, and a managed element read back as `0` is dereferenced
	// by the first thing that touches it.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE COMPILE-TIME MARK CANNOT COVER THIS, WHICH IS WHY IT IS A RUN-TIME ABORT.** `remove` marks the
	// ARGUMENT's binding moved, and that mark defends one NAME; a node has as many independently-named
	// handles as a program cares to mint (`head`/`tail`/`next`/`prev`, a field, a call result), and every one
	// of them but the marked binding reaches the emptied slot unrefused. MEASURED as **exit 139** on
	// `let h = try chain.head() … ; chain.remove(a); h.value()` — eight lines, no struct, on the pre-`BATCH37`
	// compiler too through a field copy.
	//
	// ⚠ **`element_drop@24` IS WHAT MAKES THE TEST HONEST**: a TRIVIAL element legitimately holds `0`
	// (`chain.insertLast(0)`), so the emptiness of the slot means nothing on its own. It is the same two-test
	// pair `emitDropNodeElement` makes, asked in the other order — value first, because it is the one that
	// answers on every element read of every list loop.
	//
	// ⚖ WHAT `n.value()` on an emptied node SHOULD MEAN — refuse the whole aliasing shape at the front end,
	// or make the read throw — is a semantic question `/specs/managed-list.md` does not answer and this abort
	// does not decide; like `managedListNodeNotInThisChain` beside it, it only stops the corruption.
	managedListNodeElementMovedOut = 81

	// `__slab_assert_os_alloc`: an OS page call was REFUSED — the address space or the system commit limit is
	// exhausted. Every caller stores INTO the pointer it just got, so without this guard the condition arrives
	// as `mov %reg, (<garbage>)`: an opaque access violation, in the one failure a user is most likely to hit
	// for a reason outside their own program.
	//
	// ⚠ It guards FOUR calls, not one — `osAllocPages` for the OS-direct tier and for a GT-record chunk, and
	// `osReservePages` and `osCommitPages` for the slab arena — and its test is `ptr <= 0` rather than
	// `ptr == 0`, because a raw POSIX `mmap` reports failure as a small NEGATIVE errno where `VirtualAlloc`
	// reports NULL. See the guard itself in `SlabArena.maxon` for why the sign catches both and no lane can
	// answer a page base that is not positive.
	slabOsAllocFailed = 82

	// `__slab_arena_alloc_chunks`: a chunk-run request no arena can EVER satisfy — a count of 0 or less, or
	// more chunks than an arena holds past its metadata. It is a COMPILER bug (a caller computed a span
	// geometry wrong), and both directions are silent without the guard: the loop maps a fresh arena and
	// rescans forever for a run that cannot fit, reporting out-of-memory for a request that was merely too
	// large, while a 0-chunk request satisfies `runLen >= 0` before any bit is cleared and answers a chunk
	// still marked FREE — which is then handed out a second time.
	slabChunkRunUnsatisfiable = 83

	// `__slab_arena_free_chunks`: a chunk range outside the arena. Below the metadata chunks it releases the
	// arena's own bitmap into the pool; past the last chunk it writes beyond the bitmap into chunk 0's
	// payload. Both corrupt the map that decides which chunks are free, so the fault surfaces later,
	// somewhere else, as a chunk handed out twice — which is why it aborts here instead. A separate code
	// from the one above, this registry's founding rule: two different faults in two different functions may
	// not report one number.
	slabChunkRangeOutOfArena = 84

	// `__slab_refill`: a class whose span holds NO objects was asked for one. Only class 0 — the ladder's
	// sentinel, size 0 — can answer that description, and the only request that reaches it is a ZERO-BYTE
	// one. It is refused HERE, on the refill path, rather than at the class lookup, because that lookup is
	// on the hot path of every allocation in the language and this case has no caller: a mcache row
	// for the sentinel class is always empty, so a zero-byte request always arrives here on the first try.
	// Unguarded it computes a zero-chunk span and lands on `slabChunkRunUnsatisfiable`, which is a true
	// statement about the arena and a misleading one about the fault.
	slabClassServesNoObject = 85

	// ⭐⭐ `__slab_alloc`'s pop (`emitSlabAllocClass`): **INV-1 IS BROKEN.** The span reported free slots
	// (`free_count > 0`), its free list was empty, and its virgin bump region was exhausted — so the three
	// sources of a free slot disagree about how many there are. The alternative to trapping is handing out
	// a slot PAST the span's last one, which writes into whatever the arena placed in the next chunk and
	// surfaces later, somewhere else, as one slot owned by two objects.
	slabSpanExhaustedPastItsEnd = 86

	// `__slab_os_direct_free`: a pointer whose 16-byte prefix does not carry the allocator's magic word — a
	// double free, or a pointer that never came from the OS-direct tier. The length is carried in the
	// mapping rather than in a side table, so there is nothing to search and the stamp is what catches
	// the fault. Unguarded, the free reads user data as a byte count and hands it to `osFreePages`,
	// which unmaps an arbitrary region of the process.
	slabOsDirectPointerNotOurs = 87

	// `__slab_arena_of`: an address that lies inside NO arena's reservation. Its one caller is the scavenger's
	// span destruction, which needs an arena base to turn a span's page base into a chunk index — so reaching
	// here means a span was registered whose pages did not come from the arena at all, which is a COMPILER
	// bug rather than a program's. A sentinel would be worse than useless: every caller uses the answer as
	// the base it subtracts from, so a 0 releases chunk `addr >> 13` of the arena at address 0, into a bitmap
	// that is whatever sits at address 8. Separate from `slabChunkRangeOutOfArena` beside it, this registry's
	// founding rule: that one is a bad RANGE inside a real arena, this one is no arena at all.
	slabAddressOutsideEveryArena = 88

	// ⛔⛔ `__slab_free`: a free reached a span carrying `MspanOwningPFreed` — the span has been destroyed and
	// its header is on the metadata free list, so the pointer handed in is a DOUBLE FREE or a slot that
	// outlived the span it came from. It cannot be a benign race: a span is destroyed only under
	// `__slab_lock` after its remote chain has been collected and every one of its slots accounted free, so
	// nobody who holds a live slot can see this sentinel. Left to continue, the free would CAS-push onto a
	// record another span is about to be handed.
	//
	// ⚠ It aborts for ANY caller, on a real P or on a raw OS thread: the counting argument does not depend
	// on who is asking.
	slabFreeOfParkedSpan = 89

	// ⛔⛔ `__slab_arena_new`: the arena's base is not a multiple of `SlabArenaCommitGranuleBytes`.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **THIS IS THE INVARIANT THAT WAS BROKEN FOR TWO WEEKS WITH NOTHING TO SAY SO, AND IT IS WHY THIS
	// CASE EXISTS RATHER THAN A COMMENT.** `__slab_arena_map_get` indexes by the ABSOLUTE page
	// `addr shr SlabPageShift` while the chunk grid is `arenaBase + k * SlabPageBytes`, so the two grids
	// coincide only when the base is chunk-aligned. `mmap` promises only the OS page — 4 KiB on x64 Linux,
	// HALF a chunk — so on POSIX the base was chunk-aligned about half the time, and the other half a span's
	// tail page mapped to nothing (exit 87 elsewhere) or two spans shared one map page (a corrupted free
	// list, then a SIGSEGV somewhere else). The lane read 50% green.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT LOOKS TAUTOLOGICAL AFTER `MmRuntime.emitAlignUp` AND THAT IS THE POINT.** The round-up runs on
	// every lane, so this can only fire if the round-up itself is wrong — a granule that is not a power of
	// two, or a backend materialising the `-granule` mask at the wrong WIDTH, which is a defect class this
	// project has shipped before. What the lanes differ in is the SLACK: where a reservation IS an
	// allocation (wasm32-wasi, whose `memory.grow` answers `pageIndex shl 16`) `arenaAlignSlackBytes` gives
	// ZERO, so a round-up that actually moved the base would move it PAST the reservation's end.
	// `checkArenaReservationSlack` asserts that premise at compile time from the grow unit; this asks the
	// same thing of the address a future lane really returns.
	slabArenaBaseNotGranuleAligned = 91

	// ⛔⛔ `__sched_checkdead`: a SCHEDULER DEADLOCK — Go's `checkdead` and its `"all goroutines are asleep -
	// deadlock!"`. Every machine is idle, no timer is pending, no child is parked and no overlapped read is in
	// flight, while `main` has not completed: nothing can ever make a green thread runnable again, so `main`
	// can never finish.
	//
	// ⭐ **AN `awaitAny` REACHES IT ON THE SAME ARGUMENT, K THREADS INSTEAD OF ONE.** ⚠ An **EMPTY** array
	// reaches it too, and correctly: awaiting any of zero promises can never complete.
	//
	// ⭐ **IT IS AN ABORT AND NOT A HANG**, because a deadlock is a defect in the program or in the runtime
	// and the honest response is to say so and stop.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE EXIT DRAIN DOES NOT REACH IT, AND MUST NOT.** Once `main` has completed the same finding ends
	// the drain instead (`SchedRuntime.SchedPhase.stuck`); a promise still outstanding then is a green thread
	// neither awaited nor dropped, which is `greenThreadLeak` (75) with the count in it, reported by
	// `__mm_leak_check` a few instructions later. Two different faults may not report one number.
	schedulerDeadlock = 92

	// `__gt_register_park`: the park note carries a kind it does not register. `GtRuntime`'s
	// `GtParkKindTimer` / `GtParkKindNetpoll` / `GtParkKindNetpollAny` / `GtParkKindMailbox` / `GtParkKindAwait` /
	// `GtParkKindAwaitAny` are the kinds it registers, and `GtParkKindYield` and `GtParkKindPreempt` are placed by
	// the strand runner and never reach it — so any other value is a COMPILER bug rather than a program's, and it
	// is silent without this: the thread would be suspended having been registered on NO store at all, which is a
	// hang three functions away from its cause. It is a separate code from `schedulerDeadlock` above, this registry's
	// founding rule: a runtime that cannot register a park and a scheduler that has nothing left to run are two
	// different faults.
	//
	// ⛔ The kinds are enumerated by NAME rather than counted, so that the next one has to be spelled here to
	// be believed; do not reintroduce a bare number.
	schedulerUnknownParkKind = 93

	// ⛔⛔ `__gt_promise_drop`: a PROMISE named a green thread parked in `__mbox_recv`. Its `waiting` road
	// deregisters the timer, process and in-flight-read stores and there is nothing anywhere that clears
	// `mbox.parkedGt` but the waker's own store, so falling through it would free a thread the mailbox still
	// points at — after which the next `__mbox_send` writes `ready` into freed memory and publishes a freed
	// GT. It is a COMPILER bug rather than a program's: SV2's reply cell is a promise but runs nothing
	// (`funcPtr == 0`), and `__mbox_recv`'s only callers are inside `<T>.__loop`, whose green thread no
	// promise names — `buildGtPromiseDrop` re-derives that at the site.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS A SEPARATE CODE FROM `schedulerUnknownParkKind` (93), WHICH IS THE NEIGHBOURING FAULT AND NOT
	// THIS ONE.** 93 is a park note carrying a kind no site writes; this is a kind four sites DO write,
	// reaching a reclamation path that has no answer for it. This registry's founding rule: two different
	// faults may not report one number.
	schedulerPromiseParkedOnAMailbox = 94

	// ⛔⛔⛔ `emitSchedPidlePutLocked`: a processor was being put on `__sched_pidle` WITH A NON-EMPTY RUN
	// QUEUE — `runqhead != runqtail`. **This is Go's own assertion, ported with the code it guards**
	// (`vendor/go/src/runtime/proc.go:7425`: `pidleput` throws `"pidleput: P has non-empty run queue"`),
	// and it states the invariant the idle-P list exists under: **a processor on that list has no runnable
	// work, by invariant rather than by hope.** Whoever wants to idle a processor that still holds work
	// must hand it to a machine (`handoffp`) instead of parking it.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE PARK's RING DRAIN IS WHAT SATISFIES IT, AND THIS IS WHAT MAKES THE INVARIANT CHECKABLE.** A
	// COMPILER bug rather than a program's: no Maxon program can reach it except through a scheduler that
	// idles a processor it should have handed off.
	//
	schedulerIdledAProcessorHoldingWork = 95

	// ⭐⭐ **`WalkVisitTable.__mm_walk_settle`: a COUNTED record about to CROSS to another green thread has an
	// owner outside the graph that crosses.** A send, a `spawn`'s state and a service reply MOVE their value
	// whole, and a message argument a `let` owns is LENT; a counted record belongs to one green thread, which
	// is what lets its count step plain. The static rules prove the ROOT sole; soleness is not transitive, so
	// the graph below it is walked at run time (`__sole_<T>`, `__share_<T>`), counting the references to each
	// record the graph holds. A record reached twice by two owners inside the graph crosses with both; a
	// reachable counted record with more owners than the walk meets has one left behind on this thread —
	// stepping a count the service now steps too, or free to write what a lend froze — and there is no
	// diagnostic road left: the sender's frame is already past every compile-time gate. Aborting on the
	// sender's own green thread, before anything is enqueued, is the only answer that cannot corrupt the heap.
	//
	// ⚠ It reports a NUMBER and not the type's name for this enum's founding reason: the walk is a
	// SYNTHESIZED Std function, and the abort road (`emitRuntimeAbort`) is `osExit`, not `osPanic` —
	// `mrt_panic` prints a message but exits `PanicExitCode`, which is not this code.
	transferredRecordNotSole = 96

	// ⛔ `__sched_startm`: a machine was about to be created past `SchedRuntime.SchedMaxMachines` — Go's
	// `checkmcount` and its `throw("thread exhaustion")` (`proc.go:976`), at Go's limit. A machine is an OS
	// thread with its own syscall stack and event, and none is ever freed, so a spawn arm that keeps firing
	// would otherwise run the process out of address space and fail somewhere unrelated to the cause. A
	// separate code from `schedulerDeadlock` (92), this registry's founding rule: too many machines and too
	// little work are two different faults.
	//
	// ⚠ **NO SPEC REACHES IT**: it takes ten thousand simultaneously blocked machines. It is proved by lowering
	// the limit, which a `procs: 4` service program then crosses on its third machine.
	schedulerThreadExhaustion = 97

	// ⛔ `__gt_morestack` and `__gt_spawn`: the OS REFUSED the pages for a green thread's stack. Unguarded,
	// the grower copies the old stack to `<refused base> + size` and the spawn seeds its first frame there —
	// a write through a failed allocation. A program recursing past `GtRuntime.GtMaxStackBytes` is a different
	// fault with a different answer — `panic: stack overflow`, the fault diagnostic every native lane gives an
	// OS stack run out (`BacktraceFormat.GtStackOverflowName`) — as Go reports `stack overflow` and
	// `out of memory` apart.
	greenThreadStackAllocationFailed = 99

	// ⛔ `__gt_run_main`: the main machine's scheduler loop returned for `main`'s completion, and `main`'s
	// green thread is not `completed`. The loop returns only once `__gt_runner_done` has moved
	// `__sched_phase` past `SchedPhase.mainRunning`, which it does for `main` alone and after its completion;
	// any other road would hand the exit path an ExitCode nobody wrote. A COMPILER bug rather than a
	// program's.
	mainThreadSwitchedBackUnfinished = 100

	// `__mm_leak_check`: the live allocation count was not zero at exit — a LEAK, or (a negative count)
	// an over-release. ⚠ **`Testing/SpecWorkerPool.LeakCheckExitCode` SPELLS 101 TOO, AND FOLDING IT INTO
	// THIS CASE WOULD BE WRONG.** That constant reads the exit code of a WORKER PROCESS; it is not emitted
	// by the runtime this enum describes, so pointing the harness at this case would make it look
	// single-sourced while its actual producer sat elsewhere. 101 is a PROJECT-WIDE CONVENTION with
	// several independent producers; this enum owns the runtime's, and its job here is to stop a second
	// abort taking the number.
	leakDetected = 101

	// ⛔ `__gt_register_park` and `__gt_await_any`: a promise already names an awaiter, and a second green
	// thread is registering on it. A promise holds one awaiter (`GtRuntime.GtOffAwaiter`) and moves whole, so
	// two awaiters of one promise is a COMPILER bug rather than a program's — and a silent one without this:
	// the first awaiter's registration would be overwritten and it would never be woken. A separate code from
	// `schedulerDeadlock` (92), this registry's founding rule: a waiter nobody will wake because the runtime
	// lost it and one nobody can wake because the program is stuck are two different faults.
	schedulerPromiseAwaitedTwice = 102

	// `NetpollRuntime`: a host call of the scheduler's poller failed — the poller, its break source or its
	// timer could not be created, a wait failed other than by timing out or being interrupted, or a break
	// could not be raised — or a blocking poll received a break no raise accounts for. Every idle machine's
	// sleep ends through the poller, so a scheduler whose poller is broken cannot keep a deadline and must not
	// pretend to.
	netpollFailed = 103

	// `NetpollRuntime`: a wait answered an entry from a source the poller never registered. Its identity is
	// all this runtime could act on, so an unknown one is a lost event, not one to skip.
	netpollUnknownSource = 104

	// `__sched_syscall_regain`: a machine leaving the regain waiters at shutdown was not on the list. It joined
	// under `__sched_lock` and only a delivered processor takes it off, so a miss means a hand-over the runtime
	// lost — and with it a processor.
	schedulerRegainWaiterLost = 105

	// `__gt_run_main`: the system monitor's thread could not be created — Go's `newm` failing at `sysmon`'s start.
	// The monitor is what preempts a strand holding a processor and retakes one from a blocked kernel call, so a
	// scheduler without it can starve `main` at one processor rather than fail.
	schedulerMonitorUnstarted = 106

	// `__np_pd_wait`: two green threads waited on ONE direction of one socket. A wait holds its direction word
	// from its mark until its own thread resumes — published, or ended and not yet resumed — and a second mark
	// in that span would take the word from it and lose one of the two threads, so this names the shape at the
	// instruction that produced it rather than losing a thread silently.
	netpollDoubleWait = 107

	// `__np_pd_wait` / `__np_pd_wait_any`: a direction word still named THIS thread when its wait had already
	// ended. Every road out of a wait takes the thread off each word it is published on and pays `__np_waiters`
	// back for it; a word still naming the resuming thread means one of those roads did not, and clearing it
	// would drop a published waiter WITHOUT the matching debit. A leaked `__np_waiters` is this runtime's worst
	// shape — `emitGtStoresPending` never goes false again, so `__sched_checkdead` is disarmed for the life of
	// the process and the idle tail polls for ever.
	netpollUnhookMissed = 109

	// `__np_pd_deadline`: a per-direction deadline was set on a descriptor a MULTI-SOURCE wait is parked on.
	// A group's deadline is the group's own word and rides one timer entry under the group's tag; a member's
	// `pd.rd` is 0, so the re-arm would find nothing to disarm and add a SECOND entry under a `(gt, tag)` that
	// `__gt_timer_check` admits exactly one of. No caller does this today, and this is what keeps it that way.
	netpollGroupMemberDeadline = 110

	// `__gt_subp_yield_read`, and the collect a cancelled socket transfer makes: a wait on a MINTED source ended
	// without the completion packet that is the only proof the kernel has finished with the thread's buffer and
	// its embedded OVERLAPPED. Nothing else can end such a wait — it arms no deadline, the source belongs to that
	// one operation, and a dropped promise cancels the operation rather than deregistering the park — so reaching
	// here is a RUNTIME bug, and answering a byte count instead would hand the caller bytes out of a buffer still
	// being written.
	netpollOverlappedWaitNotACompletion = 111

	// `osPipeCreate`: the host refused `FILE_SKIP_COMPLETION_PORT_ON_SUCCESS` on a child pipe's read end, so a
	// read served out of the pipe's own buffer would still post a completion packet. `__gt_subp_yield_read`
	// answers such a read out of `[bytesOut]` and gives its poll source back without waiting, so that packet
	// would land on the NEXT operation's stamp and overwrite its byte count and status — a short or wrong
	// answer from a child that ran correctly. There is no reading of the pipe that survives it, so the process
	// ends at the creation rather than at the first wrong answer.
	overlappedPipeStillPostsOnSuccess = 112

	// `__np_pd_adopt`: the poll-record table's one writer was handed an index no source can wear. The table is
	// addressed as `table + 8 * index` with only an upper bound to grow against, so a negative one names the
	// words BEFORE the table — read as a record and readied as a green thread, or overwritten with a fresh
	// record's address. `__np_pd_slot` refuses the same index on the reading side; this is the writing side of
	// that refusal, and it is an abort rather than a skip because a source with no record is a wait nothing
	// can end.
	netpollAdoptIndexIsNotASource = 113

	// `__gt_subp_wait_collect`: an idle pass found no source to wait on. An empty group means both streams are
	// done and the child's exit is learned, which the exit check turns into the reap before any idle pass — so
	// reaching here means the drain's state words disagree, and a park on the empty group is one nothing ends.
	subpCollectIdleWithNoSource = 114

	// `__np_pd_wait` / `__np_pd_wait_any`: a resumed wait found its direction word, in the life of the record it
	// marked, holding neither verdict its endings leave nor its own thread. The word is held for the waiting
	// thread until it resumes and a second wait is refused meanwhile (`netpollDoubleWait`), so another party
	// wrote a word only this thread may settle — and whatever it stood for would be lost if the resume cleared it.
	netpollHoldLost = 115

	// `__gt_run_main`, resolving `MAXON_PREEMPT` for the monitor it is about to start: the value is neither
	// `off` nor `on` (a value that fills the buffer included). The opposite policy from `MAXON_MAX_PROCS`,
	// whose malformed value falls through to the machine's count: a switch has no numeric floor to fall
	// under, and a silently ignored `MAXON_PREEMPT=of` runs a program pinned to preemption-off under
	// preemption-on — an order the case asserts, reported as honoured.
	schedulerPreemptSwitchUnreadable = 116

	// `__gt_subp_wait_collect` / `__gt_subp_release`, contending for a slot's collect interlock
	// (`GtRuntime.SubpSlotCollector`): a release under a collect still holding the slot, a second collect on a
	// slot one already holds, or a collect on a slot a release has taken — and a collect whose group wait found a
	// member's poll record outside the life the pass began in. A collect over a slot that holds no child, or
	// two collects marking one pipe word, has no answer, so it stops here, as two readers on one socket stop at
	// `netpollDoubleWait`.
	subpCollectSlotReleased = 117

	// `__managed_vacate` and `__gt_promise_field_vacate`: a promise read out of a container slot or a struct
	// field is consumed, and the slot no longer holds it — another read of the same slot was consumed first.
	// Two reads the compiler cannot prove name one slot (a
	// run-time index, two cursors, `get(i)` beside a cursor) each hold the thread; consuming the second would
	// reclaim a thread already reclaimed.
	promiseSlotConsumedTwice = 118

	// `__slab_census_tally_walk`: the walk found a heap it cannot describe — a span whose live slots do not
	// match what its free counts say, a free-set chain leaving the span or visiting one slot twice, or a mode
	// no tally implements. The table would be published as fact, so nothing is published at all.
	slabCensusTallyUnsound = 119

	// `__mm_clone_uncloneable`: a deep copy reached the clone word of a conformer nothing can duplicate — one
	// owning an OS handle, or a generic conformer with no per-declaration cloner. The front end admits a clone
	// of an interface-typed field only where every conformer of that interface can be deep-cloned, so this
	// says the roster that gate read missed one. Copying anyway would hand two owners one box.
	existentialNotCloneable = 120

	// ⚠ **108 IS VACANT, NOT REUSABLE.** The fault it would name — `__np_pd_open` / `__np_pd_release` finding
	// a green thread still PUBLISHED on a direction word of the record they are about to reset — is not a
	// fault: it rests on the false claim that no two green threads can name one socket box, where a co-owning
	// `async` argument gives a parked coroutine and a running owner one socket, and `client.close()` under a
	// parked `recv` reaches exactly that. The release READIES such a waiter instead (`NetpollRuntime`'s
	// `emitNetpollPdClear`), so nothing emits the code and a live registry may not claim the number.
	// It is left VACANT for this registry's founding reason: an exit code is read by a debugger against
	// whatever the tree said at the time, and a recycled number makes two different faults indistinguishable
	// in an old log.
end 'RuntimeAbort'

// ⭐ **THE PANIC LINE ITSELF — `panic at <file>:<line>: <text>\n` — WRITTEN ONCE.**
//
// THREE producers assemble a message for `mrt_panic`, and they must not each spell the format: the P1.9
// range-check guard (`InsertRangeChecks.rangeCheckMessage`), a user `panic("…")`
// (`Parser.parsePanicStatement`), and an INTERPOLATED `panic("…{x}…")`, which cannot call this function
// at all because its text is not known until the program runs — it takes the two halves below instead
// (`Parser.renderedPanicMessagePointer`). They differ ONLY in the TEXT after the colon — "Range check
// failed: …" against whatever the program wrote — so the location prefix, the separator and the trailing
// newline are this file's, and no caller restates them. `mrt_panic` prints the result verbatim, then
// `Stack trace:` and its frames, then exits `PanicExitCode`.
export function panicMessageLine(filePath FilePath, line LineNumber, text String) returns ByteArray
	var baked = panicMessageLinePrefix(filePath, line: line)
	baked.append(text.toByteArray())
	baked.append(PanicMessageLineTerminator)
	return baked
end 'panicMessageLine'

// ⭐ **THE LINE'S TWO CONSTANT HALVES, NAMED, because a THIRD producer needs them SEPARATELY.** An
// interpolated `panic("value is {x}")` cannot bake its line — `{x}` is unknown until the program runs —
// so the parser assembles it as an ordinary interpolation with these two as extra literal fragments
// (`Parser.renderedPanicMessagePointer`). Splitting the format here rather than letting that path spell
// `"panic at …: "` a second time is the whole reason this function exists: the location prefix, the
// separator and the terminator stay written down ONCE, whichever of the three producers assembles them.
export function panicMessageLinePrefix(filePath FilePath, line LineNumber) returns ByteArray
	return "panic at {baseName(filePath.toString())}:{line}: ".toByteArray()
end 'panicMessageLinePrefix'

// ⚠ **THE NEWLINE IS PART OF THE MESSAGE, NOT OF THE RUNTIME** — `mrt_panic` writes the cstr it is handed
// and starts the backtrace header immediately after, so a producer that omitted it would run the message
// into `Stack trace:` on one line.
export let PanicMessageLineTerminator = b"\n"

// The base file name (last path segment) of a source path, for the runtime panic message. Strips both
// separators so a Windows temp path and a POSIX one both reduce to `<name>.test`.
function baseName(path String) returns String
	return lastSegment(lastSegment(path, separator: "/"), separator: "\\")
end 'baseName'

function lastSegment(s String, separator String) returns String
	let parts = s.split(separator)

	if parts.isEmpty() 'whole'
		return s
	end 'whole'

	return try parts.get(parts.count() - 1) otherwise s
end 'lastSegment'

// ⭐ **ABORT THE PROCESS FROM A BUILDER-BUILT RUNTIME FUNCTION — THE ONE HOME OF THE ABORT SHAPE.**
// `osExit` DIVERGES, so the block it sits in never falls out; but a block must still carry a
// terminator, so a DEAD one follows. That fact was open-coded four times (`ManagedMemoryRuntime.emitVoidAbort`
// plus `GtRuntime`'s process-store append and `__gt_process_run`, and `ManagedMemoryRuntime.__managed_create`), and it
// is written here once.
//
// ⭐ **THE TERMINATOR KIND IS DERIVED FROM THE FUNCTION, NOT PASSED IN.** A void function's dead
// terminator is `retVoid` and a value-returning one's is `ret <0>`; that is exactly what
// `func.returnType` already says, so it is read rather than restated. A caller therefore cannot pick
// the wrong one — which is the failure the previous split into a void helper plus two open-coded
// value-returning copies left available. (A THROWING runtime function returns `value(i64)` through the
// dual-register ABI; its `ret` is dead too, so the same answer is right there.)
export function emitRuntimeAbort(module StdModule, func IrFunction, block IrBlock, abort RuntimeAbort, ids ValueMinter)
	// Bound to a local FIRST: the union-case argument type-checker cannot lower an
	// `EnumMember.rawValue` written directly as a case argument (E9001) — the same reason
	// `StdToArm64Conversion.vregOf` binds `reg.rawValue` before using it.
	let code = abort.rawValue as ParsedInt
	let codeId = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.const(codeId, value: code, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.osExit(codeId))

	match func.returnType 'deadTerminator'
		void then module.setTerminator(block, op: StdOp.retVoid)
		value then emitDeadValueReturn(module, block: block, ids: ids)
	end 'deadTerminator'
end 'emitRuntimeAbort'

// The dead `ret 0` a value-returning function's abort block carries. The value is unread by
// construction — `osExit` above it never returns — so a plain 0 is the honest filler. It is a function
// rather than two lines inside the arm above because a `match` arm is ONE statement.
function emitDeadValueReturn(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	let deadId = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.const(deadId, value: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(block, op: StdOp.ret(deadId))
end 'emitDeadValueReturn'
